group received a TA (20 mg)-soaked bioabsorbable dressing in both nasal cavities while 10 patients in the control group 
took saline-impregnated dressing in both nasal cavities. Nasal dressings were not removed until postoperative day 10. 
Serum cortisol, 12-hour urine cortisol, serum adrenal-corticotropic hormone (ACTH), and serum osteocalcin were measured 
preoperatively and on postoperative days 2 and 10. Serum cortisol levels were checked 1 day after surgery additionally, 
while urine cortisol levels were not checked at postoperative day 10. RESULTS All 20 patients completed this study. The 
serum cortisol levels were significantly suppressed at postoperative days 1 and 2 in the the TA group. Serum ACTH and 
12-hour urine cortisol levels were lower 2 days after surgery in the TA group, although these changes were not 
statistically significant. There were no differences in all other parameters between the TA and control groups on 
postoperative day 10. CONCLUSIONS TA-impregnated nasal dressings suppress serum cortisol levels during the early 
postoperative period. This systemic effect was recovered gradually and normalized 10 days after the operation."}, 
